# Composition of bounds across scales, continued: Theorem R_J, the one-shot loss, consumer costs

A Lean 4 / Mathlib development about how scale-by-scale bounds compose in a multiscale argument.
It continues the series from Parts 1–6, and its parts are numbered 7–9. It does not import
Parts 1–6: where a part uses one of their definitions or statements, it re-defines or re-proves
it. Each part was written as a separate module and is wrapped here in its own `section`, so that
its `open` commands stay local; the namespaces are distinct, so no names clash.

| part | section | namespace | content |
|---|---|---|---|
| 7 | `Part7_DepthChannels` | `DepthChannels` | Theorem R_J: deviation-channel families of every depth and their loss bound; depth monotonicity; the exactness and threshold criteria; the curvature floor; examples, counterexamples and witnesses |
| 8 | `Part8_OneShotLoss` | `OneShotLoss` | the one-shot loss of site-additive norms: the loss bound and its equality criterion; the negative-mass identity; bounded products and private support; absorption; the 1D scalar closed forms; assembly; the `ℓ²`-minimal section and the quotient Poincaré identity; the quotient-`ℓ¹` loss; curvature summation; examples |
| 9 | `Part9_EnergyConsumer` | `EnergyConsumer` | cost families for the local-cost comparison: the four forms of the consumer hypothesis; test fields; non-ergodic covers; the energy family as an additive-Schwarz bound; twists and their price; the multiplicity bound; value-only costs; detectors; examples |

## Main results

* **Theorem R_J** (Part 7). Let `R_k : E_{k+1} → E_k` and `V_k : E_k → E_{k+1}` be linear with
  `V_k R_k = I`, `P_k = R_k V_k`, and let `‖·‖_k` and `Γ_k` be seminorms with `‖R_k z‖ ≤ L‖z‖`,
  `Γ_k(R_k z) ≤ K‖z‖` and `Γ_k ≤ g‖·‖`, where `s > 0` and `β, L, K, g ≥ 0`. For every depth
  `J`, the family `N_0 = ‖·‖_0`,
  `N_k(y) = s^k (‖y‖_k + βΓ_k(y) + Σ_{i<J} δ_i ‖(I − P_{k+i}) V_{k+i−1} ⋯ V_k y‖)` with
  `c = L + βK + s(1 + gβ)`, `s δ_i ≥ c L^i + δ_{i+1}` (`i < J`) and `δ_J ≥ 0` composes across
  scales iff the value-only condition `‖R_0 u‖ ≤ s(‖u‖ + βΓ_1(u))` holds on
  `U^J_0 = range(R_1 ⋯ R_J)` and the two-term condition
  `‖R_k u‖ + βΓ_k(R_k u) ≤ s(‖u‖ + βΓ_{k+1}(u))` holds on `U^J_k = range(R_{k+1} ⋯ R_{k+J})` for
  every `k ≥ 1` (`thmRJ_family_iff`; explicit coefficients in `thmRJ_family_iff_std`, depth three
  in `thmR3_family_iff`). It generalises Theorems R (`J = 1`) and R₂ (`J = 2`). Under the anchor
  bound `s‖z‖ ≤ ‖R_k z‖` and `‖V_k y‖ ≤ v‖y‖`, with `δ_i, v ≥ 0`,
  `N_k(y) ≤ (1 + gβ + (1 + Lv) Σ_{i<J} δ_i v^i) ‖T_k y‖`, uniformly in `k` (`thmRJ_loss_bound`).
* **Depth monotonicity** (Part 7). `U^{J+1}_k ⊆ U^J_k`, so a family that composes at depth `J`
  composes at every larger depth with the same `β`, and suprema over the restricted spaces do not
  increase with depth (`U_succ_le`, `U_antitone`, `composes_of_le`, `sSup_image_U_le`,
  `ratioSet_antitone`, `betaStar_antitone`); the converse transfer fails
  (`nonvacuity_depth_rescue`).
* **Exactness and threshold criteria** (Part 7). With the excess `‖R_k u‖ − s‖u‖` and the gap
  `sΓ_{k+1}(u) − Γ_k(R_k u)` (`sΓ_1(u)` at the first step) on the restricted spaces, and under the
  bounds of Theorem R_J and the anchor bound: some `β ≥ 0` and `δ` make the family compose iff
  (i) a non-positive gap forces zero excess, (ii) the ratios excess/gap over positive gaps are
  bounded above, and (iii) a negative gap anywhere forces zero excess everywhere
  (`exactness_iff`); for a fixed `β` the admissible set is
  the interval cut out by these ratios (`restricted_iff_interval`). Clause (iii) cannot be
  dropped, even for linear maps satisfying every hypothesis of Theorem R_J that does not
  involve `β` or `δ`, and the anchor bound (`linear_counterexample`). If every gap is
  non-negative and a zero gap forces non-positive excess, then `β ≥ 0` is admissible iff
  `β ≥ β_*`, the least upper bound of
  `{0} ∪ {excess/gap}` (`admissible_iff_ge_threshold`, `threshold_criterion`).
* **The curvature floor** (Part 7). For curvature maps with `F_{k+1} V_k = W_k F_k` and an
  averaging map with `m‖W_k g‖ ≤ ‖g‖`: `m‖F_{k+1} u‖ ≤ ‖F_k R_k u‖`, and `m^j` over `j` steps
  (`curvature_floor`, `curvature_floor_iter`, `l1_mulVec_le`); so, for `m < s`, every `β ≥ 0`
  for which the two-term condition holds at a point `u` with `Γ_{k+1}(u) > 0` satisfies
  `β ≥ τ(u)/(s − m)`, `τ(u) = (‖R_k u‖ − s‖u‖)/Γ_{k+1}(u)` (`tau_div_le_beta`,
  `div_le_beta_ratio`, `tau_div_le_betaStar`).
* **The one-shot loss bound and its equality criterion** (Part 8). If `A T = I` and
  `‖A‖₁→₁ ≤ 1/s`, the site-additive coarse cost with weights `‖T e_w‖₁` overestimates the truth
  `y ↦ ‖T y‖₁` by at most the factor `max_w ‖T e_w‖₁ / s` (`oneShot_loss_le`). For `A ≥ 0`
  with every column sum exactly `1/s`, the bound is an equality at `y` iff `y` is supported where
  `‖T e_w‖₁` is maximal and `A` sees no sign cancellation of `T y` (`oneShot_eq_iff`); the loss
  ratio attains its supremum (`exists_max_lossRatio`), and the bound is that supremum iff such a
  `y ≠ 0` exists (`oneShot_sup_eq_iff`), for instance a sign-coherent field
  (`oneShot_sup_eq_of_nonneg`). If
  the signed column sums of `A` equal `1/s`, then `‖T e_w‖₁ / s = 1 + 2 N⁻_w / s`, with `N⁻_w` the
  negative mass of the column (`colNorm_div_eq_one_add_negMass`).
* **Bounded products, private support and absorption** (Part 8). For `a_i ≥ 0`,
  `Π_{i<k} (1 + a_i)` is bounded iff `Σ a_i < ∞` (`prod_bddAbove_iff_summable`). For `P, R ≥ 0`
  with `P R = I` and rows of `P` covering the sites, the columns of `R` live on private sites, are
  disjoint, and the box hull is exact (`private_support`, `colDisjoint_of_private`,
  `wl1_colNorm_eq_of_disjoint`). For the cube coefficient `α(N) = A_c/N + κ ℓ α̂_N`,
  `α̂_N = A(1/N − 2/(5N²))`, the gap bound `log(1/α(N))/(2N)` can be made of order `1/B`,
  `B = A_c + κℓA` (`gapBound_ge`, `exists_gapBound_ge`), and never exceeds `1/(2eB')`,
  `B' = A_c + (4/5)κℓA`, for `N ≥ 2` (`gapBound_le`). For `A_c ≥ 0` and `κ, A > 0`, a loss
  sequence `ℓ_k ≥ 1` is absorbable iff it is bounded (`absorbable_iff_bddAbove`); increments
  of at least `c/(k+1)` force `ℓ_k → ∞`, so such a loss is not absorbable
