* **The 1D scalar closed forms** (Part 8). For `ℓ_k = 1 + 2√3 r_N G_N`, `N = 2^k`
  (`scalarLoss`): `ℓ_k < 1 + √3/2` for every `k` (`scalarLoss_lt`); `ℓ_0, …, ℓ_3` are `1`, `√2`,
  `(3 + 8√6)/15` and `1 + (55√22 − 125)/231` (`scalarLoss_zero` … `scalarLoss_three`); the limit
  is `ℓ_∞ = (2 + √3 + √6 − √2)/3` (`limitProfile_isGreatest`, `one_add_limitMax`), with
  `|ℓ_k − ℓ_∞| ≤ 4·4^{−k}` and `|ℓ_{k+1} − ℓ_k| ≤ 5·4^{−k}` (`scalarLoss_rate`,
  `scalarLoss_increment`, `tendsto_scalarLoss`).
* **Assembly** (Part 8). From minimality on an affine set `φ + K`, a reference field, a one-shot
  Poincaré inequality on `K`, column decay across annuli and an annulus count:
  `‖φ‖₁/8^k ≤ 16√6 (p_R + √(q_R/c_P)) C_D (1 − e^{−λ})^{−5/2}`, a constant independent of `k`
  whenever the inputs are (`assembly_bound`, `assembly_loss_bound`).
* **The `ℓ²`-minimal section and the quotient-`ℓ¹` loss** (Part 8). For a subspace `W ≤ Σ₀`
  (the pure gauges, in the application) on which the energy and the block averages vanish,
  `Φ_W = I − P_W` maps every complementary slice
  linearly and bijectively onto `Σ' = Σ₀ ∩ W^⊥`, preserving the energy and the block averages
  (`exists_sectionEquiv`, `map_gaugeReduce`), and is Euclidean-minimal (`norm_gaugeReduce_le`); a
  constant is a one-shot Poincaré constant of `Σ'` iff it is a quotient Poincaré constant
  (`quotient_poincare_iff`), and, when the map `D` of the inequality vanishes on `W`, no section
  covering `Σ'` modulo `W` has a better one (`poincare_transfer`; `slice_covers` gives the
  covering). Modulo gauge directions, with `A(G_f) ⊆ G_c`, the
  one-shot bound holds with the quotient weights `L_w` (`quot_admissible`, `quot_loss_le`), is an
  equality at a field with a dual certificate on the maximisers of `L_w` and a gauge
  representative of small mass, for instance a flat field whose direction maximises `L_w`
  (`quot_loss_eq`), and its constant is at most that of any gauge-equivalent reconstruction
  (`quotWeights_le_colNorm`, `quot_const_le_gauge`).
* **Curvature summation** (Part 8). In dimension `d ≥ 2`, if the `ℓ¹` mass `Φ` of a field in the
  hierarchical tree section is at most its top average plus curvature fluxes `F_i` with
  multiplicities `c 2^{(d−1)i}`, and `F_i ≤ 2^{−(d−2)i} F_0`, then `Φ ≤ s a + c 2^k F_0`: the level
  sum costs no logarithm (`level_sum_bound`). Hence the one-shot loss of a column is at most
  `1 + cκ`, `κ` the curvature mass (`loss_le_one_add_curvature`), and on `(ℤ/n)⁴`, `n = 2^k N`,
  Cauchy–Schwarz over the `6n⁴` plaquettes and an energy bound `Q ≤ 1536` give `κ ≤ 96 N²`
  (`curvature_mass_le`). All three are stated as real inequalities with their hypotheses; the
  reading on trees, plaquettes and the lattice is the intended one and is not formalised.
* **The consumer forms and test fields** (Part 9). For cubes `x` with touched sets `T_x`,
  multiplicities `m_t = #{x : t ∈ T_x}` and block updates `H_x`: the representation form (for
  `ℓ¹` costs), the untouched form (H1)–(H3), the self-influence form (H1′) and the summed form
  `Σ_x Σ_{t ∈ T_x} w_t φ_t(H_x d) ≤ θ Σ_t m_t w_t φ_t(d)` each imply the next, for nonnegative
  costs and weights, the first step under the column condition (`untouched_of_representation`,
  `selfInfluence_of_untouched`, `summed_of_selfInfluence`, `summed_of_untouched`,
  `summed_of_rowWeights`); one test field bounds `θ` from below in the summed, self-influence and
  untouched forms, also after averaging spatial weights over translates
  (`ratio_le_of_summed`, `summed_of_average`, `test_field_bound`).
* **Non-ergodic covers and the energy family** (Part 9). At Gaussian order (`H_x = 1 − P_x`, with
  `P_x` the orthogonal projection onto the fibre `F_x`), if the fibres do not span the section,
  every cost family that separates fields needs `θ ≥ 1`, in the summed, self-influence and
  untouched forms (`one_le_of_not_ergodic`
  and its variants, `exists_fixed_of_not_ergodic`, `ergodic_iff_no_fixed`). For the energy family
  (the action's own local terms, squared, with the action their sum and the untouched terms
  vanishing on the fibres), `E_x(H_x d) = E_x(d) − ‖P_x d‖²` (`energy_identity`), so
  the summed form is exactly the stable-splitting (additive Schwarz) bound
  `(1 − θ) Σ_t m_t a_t (ℓ_t d)² ≤ Σ_x ‖P_x d‖²` (`energy_summedForm_iff`); `θ = 1` works on every
  cover (`energy_summedForm_one`), and, in finite dimension with `a ≥ 0` and every term touched,
  some `θ < 1` works iff the cover is ergodic, for one finite system and without uniformity
  (`energy_lt_one_iff_ergodic`); a weighted splitting bound gives the summed
  form with spatial weights (`energy_weighted`, `energy_weighted_summedForm`).
* **Twists and multiplicities** (Part 9). A twist (a field invisible to the untouched terms of a
  cube, with positive touched cost) rules out the untouched form (`not_untouchedForm_of_twist`);
  with value terms, `a > 0` and `Σ_x |T_x ∩ 𝒱| > 0`, its price in the untouched form is
  `α ≥ Σ_x η_x / (a Σ_x |T_x ∩ 𝒱|)` (`twist_lower_bound`, `twist_lower_bound_ratio`);
  in the self-influence and summed forms twists
  cost only finite constants (`selfInfluence_of_zero_set_match`, `zero_set_match_of_action`,
  `twists_cost_finite`, `energy_selfInfluenceForm`). A unit perturbation of one exterior link
  gives `θ ≥ 1 − 1/⟨m_t/m_e⟩_w` (`multiplicity_bound`, `multiplicity_bound_avg`).
* **Value-only costs and detectors** (Part 9). For single-site `ℓ¹` costs on a coordinate space,
  the optimal `θ` of the summed form is the additive constant `α_add`
  (`value_only_summedForm_iff_alphaAdd_le`); with extra terms of relative weight `b/a`, upper and
  lower bounds on the optimal `θ` approach `α_add` as `a/b → ∞` (`combined_upper`,
  `combined_lower`; the limit itself is not formalised). By the Fredholm
  alternative an exterior has a completion iff the left kernel of the interior constraints
  annihilates it (`completable_iff`); if a flat completion has zero touched cost, the resulting
  fibre-constant detectors see every twist, and finitely many of them give (H1)
  (`detectors_see_twists`, `detectors_H1`).

Every part ends with examples on concrete data that apply its main theorems; each example's
docstring says which hypotheses it lists and which of its instances are degenerate. Part 7 adds
a witness on explicit data for each theorem that its examples do not apply directly, and Part 8
one for its spline-root and shell-count lemmas.

## Scope

These are results about proof methods at Gaussian order (deviation-channel families of every
depth, the one-shot loss of site-additive norms, and the cost families that the local-cost
comparison consumes). Nothing here is about the Yang–Mills measure. The estimates (D) (the cube
estimate `D(n, α)`) and (E) (the existence of a nontrivial continuum limit satisfying the
Osterwalder–Schrader axioms with a gap in physical units) remain open, and so does the mass gap;
nothing here addresses them.

## Not formalised here

* in Part 7: Lemma F (the anchor bound `s‖z‖ ≤ ‖R_k z‖`) and the constants of the gauge
  application (`s = 8`, `Γ ≤ 6‖·‖₁`, `‖V_k‖ = 1/8`); the averaging identity `dV = W d` and the
  weights of the plaquette average that give the floor `m = 4` in four dimensions: they enter only
  as hypotheses, and the curvature floor (Lemma F2) is proved in abstract form only;
* in Part 8: the analytic inputs of the one-shot method (minimality of the reconstruction,
  Poincaré inequalities, decay, counting and energy bounds), which enter only as hypotheses; the
  form `α(N) = A_c/N + κ ℓ α̂_N` of the fine cube coefficient (with `A_c` uniform in the scale and
  `κ` an `O(1)` class-counting constant), an assumed reduction that enters as a definition, so that
  every absorption result is conditional on it; the identification of the closed form `ℓ_k` with
  the one-shot loss of the 1D chain (a discrete cubic spline); the annulus count and the column
  decay of the assembly; the complementarity `S + W = Σ₀` of the `ℓ²`-minimal section; the
  maximality of the quotient weights at a flat field, which a direction-symmetric model would
  give; and, of the curvature control of the tree-gauge loss, everything except the summation
  step and its two corollaries: the telescoping estimate along ancestors and the energy bound are
  hypotheses;
* in Part 9: the identification of the touched transport cost with `Σ_{t ∈ T_x} w_t φ_t(H_x d)` at
  Gaussian order (synchronous couplings); the Gaussian-order fact that a flat completion has zero
  touched cost; the existence of gauge-invariant (curvature) extensions of the detectors, and their
  support; the gauge invariance of the pure-gauge response in the multiplicity bound; the
  measure-theoretic half of the comparison; and every numerical value. These enter only as
  hypotheses or not at all. The size of `1 − θ` for the energy family, and its uniformity in the
  volume and the scale, are not addressed.

## Conventions

* Labels such as Theorem R_J, Lemma F, Lemma F2, (H1)–(H3), (H1′) and (Σ_w) name the mathematical
